Comparative Economic and Clinical Evaluation of SGLT2 Inhibitors vs. Standard Care in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus: A Synthetic Claims Analysis and Long-Term Markov Decision Model


Retrospective real-world evidence (RWE) derived from administrative claims data often suffers from selection bias and confounding by indication. This repository presents an end-to-end Health Economics and Outcomes Research (HEOR) workflow designed to evaluate the clinical effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of Novel SGLT2 Inhibitors (SGLT2i) compared to Standard Care (Metformin + Sulfonylurea) in patients with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM).
The analytical framework integrates:
Confounding Control: 1:1 Nearest-Neighbor Propensity Score Matching (PSM) with caliper boundaries (0.2×SD).
Econometric Modeling: Generalized Linear Models (Gamma distribution, log link) for heavily right-skewed healthcare expenditure data.
Decision-Analytic Modeling: A 20-year cohort Markov state-transition model calculating incremental cost-effectiveness ratios (ICERs) in dollars per Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY) gained.
Uncertainty Quantification: One-Way Sensitivity Analysis (OWSA Tornado Diagrams) and Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis (PSA Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curves).

Research Protocol & Study Objectives
Research Questions
Clinical Effectiveness: Does treatment with a Novel SGLT2 Inhibitor significantly reduce 1-year cardiovascular (CV) hospitalization risk relative to Standard Care in an observational claims cohort after balancing baseline clinical covariates?
Economic Evaluation: What is the 20-year Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER) of Novel SGLT2i therapy relative to Standard Care from a healthcare payer perspective?
Decision Robustness: Are the economic conclusions robust to variations in key parameters (drug acquisition cost, treatment efficacy, utility weights, and discount rates) under standard Willingness-to-Pay (WTP) thresholds ($50,000 to $100,000 / QALY)?
Formal Study Protocol Summary
Study Design: Retrospective observational claims analysis linked to a long-term Markov decision model.
Target Population: Adult patients (≥18 years) diagnosed with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.
Intervention: Novel SGLT2 Inhibitor therapy (n=454).
Comparator: Standard Care (Metformin + Sulfonylurea) (n=546).
Time Horizon: 1 Year (Claims Observational Window); 20 Years (Markov State-Transition Horizon).
Payer Perspective: Comprehensive direct medical costs (inpatient, outpatient, pharmacy claims).
Discounting: 3.0% per annum for both costs and health outcomes (QALYs).
Open-Source Healthcare Claims Data Resources
While this repository includes a fully reproducible synthetic dataset generator (set.seed(42)), the pipeline is natively structured to import real-world administrative claims datasets. Open-source claims datasets suitable for practicing this workflow include:
CMS Data Entrepreneur Synthetic Public Use Files (DE-SynPUF): Synthetic Medicare claims containing inpatient, outpatient, prescription drug, and beneficiary summary files.
Access: CMS DE-SynPUF Portal
Medical Expenditure Panel Survey (MEPS): Nationally representative survey of healthcare utilization, costs, payment sources, and diagnoses in the United States.
Access: AHRQ MEPS Open Data | R Package: MEPS
Kaggle Healthcare Claims Datasets: Open-access synthetic billing, diagnostic (ICD-10), and therapeutic claims files.
Access: Kaggle Datasets Hub
